    {
      "id": "K1wlilrGZ4VWcA5STZhA6",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -3959.669325187857,
      "y": 788.7827303886415,
      "width": 1707.273681640625,
      "height": 125.89999999999978,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "kFAzd59CB0QVDIBK9Gtof"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b3O1",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 279977225,
      "version": 728,
      "versionNonce": 1888692222,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790933317489,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Quantization-Aware Training (QAT)",
      "fontSize": 109.47826086956503,
      "fontFamily": 7,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Quantization-Aware Training (QAT)",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.15,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "fxQWUx_quwuhAku7pITLh",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -3659.57509501775,
      "y": 1125.662410100301,
      "width": 1026.778076171875,
      "height": 550,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "F8I53coFgQL0aooH7wFGr",
        "RwAhjewFBaVETu8x10NdN",
        "60McogenYCTimn7bRTCV0"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b3O2",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1184605182,
      "version": 965,
      "versionNonce": 1938939618,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790933317489,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "AI models have two life stages: training and inference.\n\nInference consists of a single forward pass through the model. inference can tolerate some loss in\nprecision, we can actually run it with quantized weights. The strategy is called post-training\nquantization, or PTQ for short. we train the model in full precision, business as usual. Then, right\nbefore deployment, we quantize the checkpoint, mapping each floating-point weight to an integer.\n\nThis is a viable solution when quantizing to higher bit widths like INT8, but it starts to break down\nwhen going as low as one or two bits. This is where quantization-aware training comes in.\n\nQAT — Quantization-Aware Training — means training the model while already making it experience the\nlow-precision version it will use later at inference time.\n\nThe model keeps two versions of its weights during training.\n- A full-precision master copy is kept for learning: gradients and optimizer updates use these precise\nweights.\n- Before each forward pass, those weights are temporarily converted into low-bit values\n- The model therefore makes predictions using the quantized weights, so it gradually learns to work well\ndespite the loss of precision.\n- After the forward pass, those temporary quantized weights are discarded; the full-precision master\nweights remain the ones that are updated.\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "AI models have two life stages: training and inference.\n\nInference consists of a single forward pass through the model. inference can tolerate some loss in precision, we can actually run it with quantized weights. The strategy is called post-training quantization, or PTQ for short. we train the model in full precision, business as usual. Then, right before deployment, we quantize the checkpoint, mapping each floating-point weight to an integer.\n\nThis is a viable solution when quantizing to higher bit widths like INT8, but it starts to break down when going as low as one or two bits. This is where quantization-aware training comes in.\n\nQAT — Quantization-Aware Training — means training the model while already making it experience the low-precision version it will use later at inference time.\n\nThe model keeps two versions of its weights during training.\n- A full-precision master copy is kept for learning: gradients and optimizer updates use these precise weights.\n- Before each forward pass, those weights are temporarily converted into low-bit values\n- The model therefore makes predictions using the quantized weights, so it gradually learns to work well despite the loss of precision.\n- After the forward pass, those temporary quantized weights are discarded; the full-precision master weights remain the ones that are updated.\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
