    {
      "id": "F_fGJXnpQR_SmOppSL9y9",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -3446.700975497565,
      "y": 2622.382405837377,
      "width": 517.9682006835938,
      "height": 41.4,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "1DLorBCSe47-l9wETIsu9"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b3R0V",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 169466914,
      "version": 212,
      "versionNonce": 755090786,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1790933317489,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Straight-Through Estimator (STE)",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "fontFamily": 7,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Straight-Through Estimator (STE)",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.15
    },
    {
      "id": "pl6niOolmFX7xYvE32NxK",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -3681.7566802501688,
      "y": 2763.2157391707096,
      "width": 1026.778076171875,
      "height": 1000,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "dLX5WcLbOfDdzd8fUYQfu",
        "sx7Gu4mfFUh99CoJ9ksc8",
        "pN4F6ze-6nxB1F--TUYV6",
        "1DLorBCSe47-l9wETIsu9"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b3R1",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1837047906,
      "version": 1169,
      "versionNonce": 1894857150,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790933317489,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "The tricky part is that quantization uses operations such as rounding, which are not normally\ndifferentiable. QAT gets around this using the Straight-Through Estimator (STE): during\nbackpropagation, it essentially treats the rounding operation as if it were passable for gradient\ncomputation\n\nRounding is a non-differentiable function, therefore this will break backpropagation, so we have to do\nsomething to fix this!\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThat would mean no gradient reaches the full-precision weight, so the model could not learn through the\nquantization operation. This is where STE is introduced.\n\nDuring the backward pass, we pretend that rounding behaves like the identity function:\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThis is the central idea of the Straight-Through Estimator: the forward pass uses the quantized value,\nbut the backward pass lets the gradient pass straight through the non-differentiable rounding\noperation.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "The tricky part is that quantization uses operations such as rounding, which are not normally differentiable. QAT gets around this using the Straight-Through Estimator (STE): during backpropagation, it essentially treats the rounding operation as if it were passable for gradient computation\n\nRounding is a non-differentiable function, therefore this will break backpropagation, so we have to do something to fix this!\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThat would mean no gradient reaches the full-precision weight, so the model could not learn through the quantization operation. This is where STE is introduced.\n\nDuring the backward pass, we pretend that rounding behaves like the identity function:\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThis is the central idea of the Straight-Through Estimator: the forward pass uses the quantized value, but the backward pass lets the gradient pass straight through the non-differentiable rounding operation.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
