        1,
        1
      ],
      "crop": null
    },
    {
      "id": "y5oJnsxOURqZ0zCYTt4rS",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -3713.494728099738,
      "y": 4116.884350231718,
      "width": 1026.778076171875,
      "height": 875,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 40,
      "groupIds": [
        "t4xI4C0e_Co_xovHckXFs",
        "Dl4hdjFZZJI3h-k6i2p01",
        "gcQPBlNpLEfLib9xMO-Mv",
        "Z2kusg6BdtTecY1slvZ-z"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b3R7",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1654228414,
      "version": 1317,
      "versionNonce": 31800190,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790933320168,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Given a fixed training budget (in #tokens) how should the budget be optimally allocated between full-\nprecision training and quantization-aware training/finetuning to maximize the accuracy of the quantized\nmodel?\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nQAT finetuning consistently surpasses both PTQ with BFPT = Btrain and QAT from scratch with\nBQAT = Btrain. Optimal performance is nearly achieved by dedicating the majority of the training budget\nto full precision (FP) training and approximately 10% to QAT.\n\nQuantization makes inference more efficient, but not necessarily training. If anything, fake quantization\nadds a little bit of overhead to the training process.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Given a fixed training budget (in #tokens) how should the budget be optimally allocated between full-precision training and quantization-aware training/finetuning to maximize the accuracy of the quantized model?\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nQAT finetuning consistently surpasses both PTQ with BFPT = Btrain and QAT from scratch with\nBQAT = Btrain. Optimal performance is nearly achieved by dedicating the majority of the training budget to full precision (FP) training and approximately 10% to QAT.\n\nQuantization makes inference more efficient, but not necessarily training. If anything, fake quantization adds a little bit of overhead to the training process.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "3kaZ6zj0mlm35eJmfkgTE",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 208.02294825371428,
      "y": 2731.7358470553454,
      "width": 1026.778076171875,
      "height": 2275,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "HlCKV87nZpjAVEg3URdKW"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b4q",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 512760226,
      "version": 2057,
      "versionNonce": 466987810,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790933799505,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Consider a concrete deployment where you want to serve a 13B LLM (e.g. Meta’s Llama 2 13B) on AWS.\n\nAWS’s g5.2xlarge instance has one NVIDIA A10G GPU with 24 GB of VRAM. AWS has published an on-\ndemand example price of $1.212/hour.\n\n                                          1.212 × 24 × 30 = $872.64/month\n\nOur model in full precision (FP32) need approximately:\n\n                                                  13B × 4bytes = 52 GB\n\nBut our GPU has only 24 GB :(\n\nSo we quantize to a lower precision (FP32 → INT1) so that each parameter requires less bytes. Moving\nfrom FP16 weights to 1-bit weights can, in principle, reduce weight storage by roughly 32×\n\nLet's say we quantize so that each parameter now need 1byte:\n\n                                                   13B × 1/8bytes < 2 GB !\n\nNow the model can realistically fit.\n\nThe second advantage is indeed cheaper arithmetic. With binary or ternary weights, expensive floating-\npoint multiplications can be replaced largely by much cheaper operations such as additions, subtractions,\nand sign handling.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nBitNet b1.58 retains all the benefits of the original\n1-bit BitNet, including its new computation paradigm, which requires almost no multiplication\noperations for matrix multiplication and can be highly optimized. Additionally, it has the same energy\nconsumption as the original 1-bit BitNet and is much more efficient in terms of memory consumption,\nthroughput and latency compared to FP16 LLM baselines.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThese developments also enable local LLM deployment across a broad range of devices. In this work, we\nintroduce bitnet.cpp , a tailored software stack designed to unlock the full potential of 1-bit LLMs.\nSpecifically, we develop a set of kernels to support fast and lossless inference of ternary BitNet b1.58\nLLMs on CPUs.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Consider a concrete deployment where you want to serve a 13B LLM (e.g. Meta’s Llama 2 13B) on AWS.\n\nAWS’s g5.2xlarge instance has one NVIDIA A10G GPU with 24 GB of VRAM. AWS has published an on-demand example price of $1.212/hour.\n\n                                          1.212 × 24 × 30 = $872.64/month\n\nOur model in full precision (FP32) need approximately:\n\n                                                  13B × 4bytes = 52 GB\n\nBut our GPU has only 24 GB :(\n\nSo we quantize to a lower precision (FP32 → INT1) so that each parameter requires less bytes. Moving from FP16 weights to 1-bit weights can, in principle, reduce weight storage by roughly 32×\n\nLet's say we quantize so that each parameter now need 1byte:\n\n                                                   13B × 1/8bytes < 2 GB !\n\nNow the model can realistically fit.\n\nThe second advantage is indeed cheaper arithmetic. With binary or ternary weights, expensive floating-point multiplications can be replaced largely by much cheaper operations such as additions, subtractions, and sign handling.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nBitNet b1.58 retains all the benefits of the original\n1-bit BitNet, including its new computation paradigm, which requires almost no multiplication\noperations for matrix multiplication and can be highly optimized. Additionally, it has the same energy\nconsumption as the original 1-bit BitNet and is much more efficient in terms of memory consumption,\nthroughput and latency compared to FP16 LLM baselines.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThese developments also enable local LLM deployment across a broad range of devices. In this work, we introduce bitnet.cpp , a tailored software stack designed to unlock the full potential of 1-bit LLMs. Specifically, we develop a set of kernels to support fast and lossless inference of ternary BitNet b1.58 LLMs on CPUs.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "uYg4fk-0x65NT0OUUkvMR",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 406.1473989373053,
      "y": 2634.635847055346,
      "width": 759.6363525390625,
      "height": 41.4,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "HlCKV87nZpjAVEg3URdKW"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b4r",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1247843682,
      "version": 1337,
      "versionNonce": 2079848162,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790933799505,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Why using quantization -> $$$, speed and energy",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "fontFamily": 7,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Why using quantization -> $$$, speed and energy",
