    {
      "id": "idE5OyRXH3EaIIPvTlYMb",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 150.21167338461373,
      "y": 6462.7500439257965,
      "width": 1142.461873372396,
      "height": 1200,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#000000",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "9V-JSPoW9dnUOZl7J0mrv",
        "mOvp-huW-XYP7tGJHHSam",
        "gT6AXYuy0Crh5LFIuWfcS"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5CO",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 496379966,
      "version": 803,
      "versionNonce": 2135451106,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790933799506,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "BitNet does not redesign the Transformer. It replaces the expensive linear layers with a new low-bit layer called\nBitLinear. The rest of the Transformer structure remains largely familiar.\n\nThe normalized activation is then quantized with Absmax quantization, so instead of using full-precision activations,\nBitLinear uses a low-precision representation.\n\nThe quantized activation and the binary weights are then combined in the matrix multiplication.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nBitNet a4.8 does not keep all activations at 8-bit. It uses 4-bit activations where possible and 8-bit where needed.\n\nFor the inputs to the attention and feed-forward layers, they found that 4-bit quantization is viable. So after\nRMSNorm, those activations are mapped to 4-bit values before entering the expensive linear computation.\n\nAttention also uses mixed activation precision.\n\nThe harder part is the intermediate activations. These tend to have stronger outliers. Instead of forcing those\ndirectly into 4 bits, BitNet a4.8 first applies Top-K sparsification: keep only the most important, high-magnitude\nactivations and set many of the others to zero. Then the remaining intermediate state is quantized to 8 bits,\nwhich provides more numerical range.\n\nThis is why the model is called a4.8: it uses a mixture of 4-bit and 8-bit activations, rather than insisting on 4 bits\neverywhere. arXiv",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "BitNet does not redesign the Transformer. It replaces the expensive linear layers with a new low-bit layer called BitLinear. The rest of the Transformer structure remains largely familiar.\n\nThe normalized activation is then quantized with Absmax quantization, so instead of using full-precision activations, BitLinear uses a low-precision representation.\n\nThe quantized activation and the binary weights are then combined in the matrix multiplication.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nBitNet a4.8 does not keep all activations at 8-bit. It uses 4-bit activations where possible and 8-bit where needed.\n\nFor the inputs to the attention and feed-forward layers, they found that 4-bit quantization is viable. So after RMSNorm, those activations are mapped to 4-bit values before entering the expensive linear computation.\n\nAttention also uses mixed activation precision.\n\nThe harder part is the intermediate activations. These tend to have stronger outliers. Instead of forcing those directly into 4 bits, BitNet a4.8 first applies Top-K sparsification: keep only the most important, high-magnitude activations and set many of the others to zero. Then the remaining intermediate state is quantized to 8 bits, which provides more numerical range.\n\nThis is why the model is called a4.8: it uses a mixture of 4-bit and 8-bit activations, rather than insisting on 4 bits everywhere. arXiv",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
