      "text": "Rounding Strategy",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 7,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Rounding Strategy",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.15,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "31XR73r6szOQIFcWrLrox",
      "type": "image",
      "x": 9040.944911940845,
      "y": 11108.918468327114,
      "width": 741,
      "height": 274,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "transparent",
      "backgroundColor": "#69db7c",
      "fillStyle": "cross-hatch",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "c65o9d6y5uIGUrDYpP6Qx"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bCi",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 185486974,
      "version": 268,
      "versionNonce": 1662985022,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1791236337553,
      "created": 1791233905135,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "status": "saved",
      "fileId": "e9265c7d47fda15649dbe5629603e2ec62bbbb3c",
      "scale": [
        1,
        1
      ],
      "crop": null
    },
    {
      "id": "WfwmabRhzRX_gQTXp0ZEa",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 8692.117384108496,
      "y": 11442.455959475827,
      "width": 678.399373372396,
      "height": 50,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#000000",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "9jF2cMFDFln9ZHSy-utvf",
        "pOF589iYliDu2ZxpOIh4Y",
        "xOvxgI8QMLO8fKm7Fqyu4",
        "c65o9d6y5uIGUrDYpP6Qx"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bCj",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1266405054,
      "version": 1334,
      "versionNonce": 1007946622,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1791236337553,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Round to nearest is deterministic: given the same number it always\nproduces the same result. round to nearest, which rounds to x_2",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Round to nearest is deterministic: given the same number it always produces the same result. round to nearest, which rounds to x_2",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "created": 1791233963161,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "A6cX1kwmDrYCME6sCI8ms",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 8861.785820794694,
      "y": 11034.568459475824,
      "width": 1090.6250000000005,
      "height": 1100,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#69db7c",
      "fillStyle": "cross-hatch",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "c65o9d6y5uIGUrDYpP6Qx"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bCk",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1523533566,
      "version": 266,
      "versionNonce": 784460734,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1791236337553,
      "created": 1791234018082,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "The following diagram shows the situation where x lies just beyond the half-way point between x_1 and x_2:7\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nIn certain situations, round to nearest produces correlated rounding errors that cause systematic error\ngrowth. This can happen when we form the inner product of two long vectors x and y of nonnegative elements.\nIf the elements all lie on [0,1] then clearly the partial sum can grow monotonically as more and more terms are\naccumulated until at some point all the remaining terms “drop off the end” of the computed sum and do not\nchange it—the sum stagnates. Stochastic rounding avoids stagnation by rounding up rather than down some\nof the time. \n\nThe next figure gives an example. Here, x and y are n-vectors sampled from the uniform [0,1] distribution, the\ninner product is computed in IEEE standard half precision floating-point arithmetic and the backward errors\nare plotted for increasing n. From about n  = 1000, the errors for stochastic rounding (SR, in orange) are\nsmaller than those for round to nearest (RTN, in blue), the latter quickly reaching 1.\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "The following diagram shows the situation where x lies just beyond the half-way point between x_1 and x_2:7\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nIn certain situations, round to nearest produces correlated rounding errors that cause systematic error growth. This can happen when we form the inner product of two long vectors x and y of nonnegative elements. If the elements all lie on [0,1] then clearly the partial sum can grow monotonically as more and more terms are accumulated until at some point all the remaining terms “drop off the end” of the computed sum and do not change it—the sum stagnates. Stochastic rounding avoids stagnation by rounding up rather than down some of the time. \n\nThe next figure gives an example. Here, x and y are n-vectors sampled from the uniform [0,1] distribution, the inner product is computed in IEEE standard half precision floating-point arithmetic and the backward errors are plotted for increasing n. From about n  = 1000, the errors for stochastic rounding (SR, in orange) are smaller than those for round to nearest (RTN, in blue), the latter quickly reaching 1.\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "u3pIyFx10HIQfJgaUH5Ks",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 9408.791814120927,
      "y": 11451.86620620857,
      "width": 678.399373372396,
      "height": 275,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#000000",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "TKvIsfn7JQkGCjuMoJgXb",
        "xubOrz24HrtcAfQNCKvp3",
        "RGtlgZwKlGIpfYoa40HXy",
        "c65o9d6y5uIGUrDYpP6Qx"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bCl",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1502197566,
      "version": 1374,
      "versionNonce": 155829246,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1791236337553,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "We round up to x_2 with probability (x-x_1)/(x_2-x_1) and down to\nx_1 with probability (x_2-x)/(x_2-x_1); If stochastic rounding\nchooses to round to x_1 in the diagram then it has committed a\nlarger error than round to nearest, which rounds to x_2. What, then,\nis the benefit of stochastic rounding?\n\nIt is not hard to show that the expected value of the result of\nstochastically rounding x is x itself (this is obvious if x =\n(x_1+x_2)/2, for example), so the expected error is zero. Hence\nstochastic rounding maintains, in a statistical sense, some of the\ninformation that is discarded by a deterministic rounding scheme. ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "We round up to x_2 with probability (x-x_1)/(x_2-x_1) and down to x_1 with probability (x_2-x)/(x_2-x_1); If stochastic rounding chooses to round to x_1 in the diagram then it has committed a larger error than round to nearest, which rounds to x_2. What, then, is the benefit of stochastic rounding?\n\nIt is not hard to show that the expected value of the result of stochastically rounding x is x itself (this is obvious if x = (x_1+x_2)/2, for example), so the expected error is zero. Hence stochastic rounding maintains, in a statistical sense, some of the information that is discarded by a deterministic rounding scheme. ",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "created": 1791234041782,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "bPvScCdCQAFyNxxZVfsBA",
      "type": "image",
      "x": 9127.15816747379,
      "y": 12148.666506860438,
      "width": 531.25,
      "height": 438.7837837837838,
