    {
      "id": "gR9UPY1GNr6vQWOW58McN",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 8858.394316702752,
      "y": 8823.402106212345,
      "width": 1142.461873372396,
      "height": 1600,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#000000",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "T8_62tyZ5MXAM0dF3Y0L8",
        "ycwrNF_pL92xKOc51r2e_",
        "91VBaFVAqBrYdISMCULr5",
        "tgPrfDKmJhTIr0_nUeDr3"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bCs",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 268036350,
      "version": 1214,
      "versionNonce": 1064029630,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1791236337553,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Imagine quantizing an entire weight matrix. One option is to find one scale for the whole matrix and then quantize\nevery element using that same (s). This is per-tensor quantization. But different regions of a large weight matrix\nmay have very different numerical ranges. A single scale therefore wastes precision. Microscaling instead divides the\nmatrix into blocks:\n\nSmaller blocks generally provide better numerical accuracy because each scale describes fewer values. However, they\nalso introduce additional metadata because more scaling factors need to be stored. This creates a trade-off.\nMicroscaling formats are designed around this compromise.\n\nTwo important FP4 microscaling formats are:\n    - MXFP4;\n    - NVFP4.\n\nMXFP4 typically groups 32 FP4 values together.\nFP4 values together.\n\nEach value uses an E2M1 representation:\n\n    - 1 sign bit;\n    - 2 exponent bits;\n    - 1 mantissa bit.\n\nThe block shares a separate scaling factor. The scale itself uses an exponent-oriented representation designed to\nprovide a large dynamic range. E8MO uses all the bits for the exponents, no sign no mantissa\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nNVIDIA's NVFP4 uses the same E2M1 representation for the actual FP4 values, but uses smaller blocks and a\ndifferent representation for the scale. An E4M3\n\n- 1 sign bit;\n    - 4 exponent bits;\n    - 3 mantissa bit.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe FP4 paper finds NVFP4 particularly effective. The Tensor Core hardware can work directly with these\nmicroscaling formats, meaning that the scaling information can be incorporated into the matrix operation without\nrequiring a separate explicit scaling operation for every element.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Imagine quantizing an entire weight matrix. One option is to find one scale for the whole matrix and then quantize every element using that same (s). This is per-tensor quantization. But different regions of a large weight matrix may have very different numerical ranges. A single scale therefore wastes precision. Microscaling instead divides the matrix into blocks:\n\nSmaller blocks generally provide better numerical accuracy because each scale describes fewer values. However, they also introduce additional metadata because more scaling factors need to be stored. This creates a trade-off. Microscaling formats are designed around this compromise.\n\nTwo important FP4 microscaling formats are:\n    - MXFP4;\n    - NVFP4.\n\nMXFP4 typically groups 32 FP4 values together.\nFP4 values together.\n\nEach value uses an E2M1 representation:\n\n    - 1 sign bit;\n    - 2 exponent bits;\n    - 1 mantissa bit.\n\nThe block shares a separate scaling factor. The scale itself uses an exponent-oriented representation designed to provide a large dynamic range. E8MO uses all the bits for the exponents, no sign no mantissa\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nNVIDIA's NVFP4 uses the same E2M1 representation for the actual FP4 values, but uses smaller blocks and a different representation for the scale. An E4M3\n\n- 1 sign bit;\n    - 4 exponent bits;\n    - 3 mantissa bit.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe FP4 paper finds NVFP4 particularly effective. The Tensor Core hardware can work directly with these microscaling formats, meaning that the scaling information can be incorporated into the matrix operation without requiring a separate explicit scaling operation for every element.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "created": 1791232025668,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
