      "updated": 1791268424742,
      "created": 1791238048708,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "FP8 Formats for Deep Learning",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 7,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "FP8 Formats for Deep Learning",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.15,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "Bzuu-WZ2I_KUuwM5UujLR",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 6225.78593525376,
      "y": 4963.816981101911,
      "width": 1142.461873372396,
      "height": 1000,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#000000",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "jxMWYsB0R3Wv3NMzr91fp",
        "Rb9wyNfeMD0UuMs6V6iDS",
        "3WmnNtTKhj40dEoCKegZX",
        "zLttCyz7MFc0s8reHHqMr"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bD4",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1283494782,
      "version": 1122,
      "versionNonce": 269836094,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1791268424742,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "The key insight was not simply that FP16 is faster. The deeper idea was that different parts of training have\ndifferent numerical requirements.\n\nMoving from FP16 to FP8 makes this problem much more severe.\n\nWith only eight bits available, we have to decide how many bits should represent:\n\n    - the sign;\n    - the exponent;\n    - the mantissa.\n\nThe exponent determines dynamic range.\nThe mantissa determines precision.\n\nAnd here we encounter a fundamental trade-off:\nAnd here we encounter a fundamental trade-off:\n\n    more exponent bits -> more range, less precision\n    more mantissa bits -> more precision, less range\n\nThe crucial observation of the FP8 paper is that different tensors need different sides of this trade-off.\n\nWeights and activations generally benefit from higher precision.\nGradients, on the other hand, can span a much wider range of magnitudes and therefore require more exponent bits.\n\nThis motivates the central innovation of the paper. This motivates the central innovation of the paper Do not use\none FP8 format. Use two complementary FP8 formats.\n\nThe authors propose two FP8 encodings:\n    - E4M3, with four exponent bits and three mantissa bits,\n    - E5M2, with five exponent bits and two mantissa bits.\n\nE4M3 gives more precision while E5M2 sacrifices one mantissa bit in exchange for a larger dynamic range:\n\nIt follows directly from the numerical characteristics of the tensors encountered during training.\n\nWeights and activations usually occupy relatively controlled distributions, so additional mantissa precision is valuable.\n\nGradients can contain both extremely small and relatively large values, particularly during backward propagation, so\ndynamic range becomes more important than fine precision.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "The key insight was not simply that FP16 is faster. The deeper idea was that different parts of training have different numerical requirements.\n\nMoving from FP16 to FP8 makes this problem much more severe.\n\nWith only eight bits available, we have to decide how many bits should represent:\n\n    - the sign;\n    - the exponent;\n    - the mantissa.\n\nThe exponent determines dynamic range.\nThe mantissa determines precision.\n\nAnd here we encounter a fundamental trade-off:\nAnd here we encounter a fundamental trade-off:\n\n    more exponent bits -> more range, less precision\n    more mantissa bits -> more precision, less range\n\nThe crucial observation of the FP8 paper is that different tensors need different sides of this trade-off.\n\nWeights and activations generally benefit from higher precision.\nGradients, on the other hand, can span a much wider range of magnitudes and therefore require more exponent bits.\n\nThis motivates the central innovation of the paper. This motivates the central innovation of the paper Do not use one FP8 format. Use two complementary FP8 formats.\n\nThe authors propose two FP8 encodings:\n    - E4M3, with four exponent bits and three mantissa bits,\n    - E5M2, with five exponent bits and two mantissa bits.\n\nE4M3 gives more precision while E5M2 sacrifices one mantissa bit in exchange for a larger dynamic range:\n\nIt follows directly from the numerical characteristics of the tensors encountered during training.\n\nWeights and activations usually occupy relatively controlled distributions, so additional mantissa precision is valuable.\n\nGradients can contain both extremely small and relatively large values, particularly during backward propagation, so dynamic range becomes more important than fine precision.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "created": 1791266117119,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "TG2vo8qGmOs-B24FYHRpq",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 6639.771416871226,
      "y": 6090.275314435246,
      "width": 229.5720977783203,
      "height": 41.4,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#69db7c",
      "fillStyle": "cross-hatch",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bD5",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 274559934,
      "version": 112,
      "versionNonce": 1726138238,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1791268424742,
      "created": 1791266905769,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Scaling Factors",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 7,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Scaling Factors",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.15,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "8MJ5PdxDB1hwYfpOs98KG",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 6220.415480185027,
      "y": 6168.816981101911,
      "width": 1142.461873372396,
      "height": 725,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#000000",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "o95LZTXGLdw2coyD_pBuX",
        "cSnfW1QLJGSVDDdZsy5rW",
        "raceBOFpPwvAW3Q2_3Oe6",
        "tBUgD5qwh_2BlHvGqZZqd"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bD6",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1043614718,
      "version": 1250,
      "versionNonce": 140060606,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1791268424742,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Before converting a higher-precision tensor into FP8, it is multiplied by a scaling factor so that its important\nvalues occupy the FP8 representable range.\n\nHowever, some networks require per-tensor scaling factors as FP8 dynamic range is not sufficient to cover the union\nof all tensors’ important values\n\nthe general idea is to choose a scaling factor such that the maximum magnitude in the tensor becomes close to the\nmaximum representable magnitude in the corresponding format. Values that overflow are then saturated to the\nmaximum representable value.\n\nValues in higher precision get unscaled by multiplying with the inverse of the scaling factor, either after conversion\nfrom FP8 or after arithmetic instructions for a linear operation have produced a higher-precision output. In both\ncases only a minimal amount of additional arithmetic is required. For matrix multiplications, unscaling is applied once\nper dot-product, thus amortized by many multiply-accumulates with FP8 inputs. Less arithmetic intensive operations\n(such as nonlinearities, normalizations, or weight updates by optimizers) are typically memory-bandwidth limited and\nnot sensitive to one additional arithmetic instruction per value.\n\nWhile training and inference for a number of networks can be successfully carried out in FP8 with the same scaling\nfactor for all the tensors of the same type. This need becomes more pronounced when we store more of the\ntensors in FP8, not just inputs for GEMM operations.\n\nAs we can see, when casting to FP8 only the inputs to GEMM operations (both weighted GEMMs as wells as two\nattention batched matrix multiplies that involve only activations), several exponent bias choices in the [7, 10] range\nlead to results matching the bfloat16 baseline.\n\nalso quantize to FP8 the residual connections hen no single exponent bias\nvalue leads to sufficient accuracy - even exponent bias of 7 results in perplexity of 12.59 which is significantly\nhigher\n(worse) than 10.19 for the bfloat16 baseline.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Before converting a higher-precision tensor into FP8, it is multiplied by a scaling factor so that its important values occupy the FP8 representable range.\n\nHowever, some networks require per-tensor scaling factors as FP8 dynamic range is not sufficient to cover the union of all tensors’ important values\n\nthe general idea is to choose a scaling factor such that the maximum magnitude in the tensor becomes close to the maximum representable magnitude in the corresponding format. Values that overflow are then saturated to the maximum representable value.\n\nValues in higher precision get unscaled by multiplying with the inverse of the scaling factor, either after conversion\nfrom FP8 or after arithmetic instructions for a linear operation have produced a higher-precision output. In both cases only a minimal amount of additional arithmetic is required. For matrix multiplications, unscaling is applied once per dot-product, thus amortized by many multiply-accumulates with FP8 inputs. Less arithmetic intensive operations (such as nonlinearities, normalizations, or weight updates by optimizers) are typically memory-bandwidth limited and not sensitive to one additional arithmetic instruction per value.\n\nWhile training and inference for a number of networks can be successfully carried out in FP8 with the same scaling\nfactor for all the tensors of the same type. This need becomes more pronounced when we store more of the tensors in FP8, not just inputs for GEMM operations.\n\nAs we can see, when casting to FP8 only the inputs to GEMM operations (both weighted GEMMs as wells as two attention batched matrix multiplies that involve only activations), several exponent bias choices in the [7, 10] range lead to results matching the bfloat16 baseline.\n\nalso quantize to FP8 the residual connections hen no single exponent bias\nvalue leads to sufficient accuracy - even exponent bias of 7 results in perplexity of 12.59 which is significantly higher\n(worse) than 10.19 for the bfloat16 baseline.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "created": 1791266917865,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "CvQXWBxH-8Q17rb433WL3",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 6453.813083537891,
      "y": 7124.962423466342,
      "width": 701.8922119140625,
      "height": 41.4,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#69db7c",
      "fillStyle": "cross-hatch",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "QL4Dj1YZzC8EgxPQawemr"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bD7",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1946557502,
      "version": 169,
      "versionNonce": 614039550,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1791268424742,
      "created": 1791267490820,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "FP8-LM: Training FP8 Large Language Models",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 7,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "FP8-LM: Training FP8 Large Language Models",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.15,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "7afgsmRraKLjetu7EUgLu",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 6245.839308947252,
      "y": 7223.996440076895,
      "width": 1142.461873372396,
      "height": 1400,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#000000",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "DDFZbzMh5a54nFg_TKG_-",
        "cVZyzgZG_io71Cz8qnCM8",
        "qTSQfRNSn_MN0D41_KBH1",
        "hXACW-P-1DQHhmwwUdeI4",
        "-FGxf6QX3mBsXjeo-6liQ",
        "QL4Dj1YZzC8EgxPQawemr"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bD8",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1739842686,
      "version": 1417,
      "versionNonce": 1067765822,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1791268424742,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "FP8-LM adopts the FP8 datatype family established by Micikevicius et al. and focuses on extending FP8 beyond\nGEMM computation to gradients, optimizer states, communication, and distributed LLM training.\n\nthe loss curves almost\noverlap with each other. The results unequivocally demonstrate that the proposed FP8 mixed-precision\nscheme can achieve equivalent performance to the prevalent higher-precision BF16 scheme\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nAlso, we evaluate the pre-trained models on a wide range of downstream tasks\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nFurthermore, we leverage the proposed FP8 mixed-precision approach for fine-tuning LLMs in instruction\nfollowing. the curves corresponding to BF16 and FP8 display a notable degree\nof overlap. we further apply the proposed FP8 mixed-precision scheme to reinforcement learning from\nhuman feedback (RLHF),\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nTheoretically, this results in a 75% reduction in communication costs when compared to the mainstream 32-bit\nscheme. When training GPT models with identical batch sizes, FP8 mixed-precision can lead to a reduction in memory\nfootprint ranging from 28% to 39% when compared to BF16\nMoreover, our FP8 mixed-precision scheme shows a superior training throughput compared to the prevalent\nBF16 scheme, achieving a notable speed-up of 75% when applied to GPT-175B model. The model FLOPS\nutilization (MFU) of FP8 mixed-precision training is 34.2% on H100 GPUs\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "FP8-LM adopts the FP8 datatype family established by Micikevicius et al. and focuses on extending FP8 beyond GEMM computation to gradients, optimizer states, communication, and distributed LLM training.\n\nthe loss curves almost\noverlap with each other. The results unequivocally demonstrate that the proposed FP8 mixed-precision\nscheme can achieve equivalent performance to the prevalent higher-precision BF16 scheme\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nAlso, we evaluate the pre-trained models on a wide range of downstream tasks\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nFurthermore, we leverage the proposed FP8 mixed-precision approach for fine-tuning LLMs in instruction\nfollowing. the curves corresponding to BF16 and FP8 display a notable degree\nof overlap. we further apply the proposed FP8 mixed-precision scheme to reinforcement learning from\nhuman feedback (RLHF),\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nTheoretically, this results in a 75% reduction in communication costs when compared to the mainstream 32-bit scheme. When training GPT models with identical batch sizes, FP8 mixed-precision can lead to a reduction in memory\nfootprint ranging from 28% to 39% when compared to BF16\nMoreover, our FP8 mixed-precision scheme shows a superior training throughput compared to the prevalent\nBF16 scheme, achieving a notable speed-up of 75% when applied to GPT-175B model. The model FLOPS\nutilization (MFU) of FP8 mixed-precision training is 34.2% on H100 GPUs\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "created": 1791267745874,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
